Testicular Torsion (Post-op)
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
Status post orchiopexy, Resolving severe scrotal pain, Healing scrotal surgical incision, Normal robust Doppler flow

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-045
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of pain and swelling at the surgical site four days post-orchiopexy, requiring immediate assessment to rule out a postoperative haematoma or rapidly spreading surgical site infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a young male, four days status post urgent bilateral testicular orchiopexy, experiencing a sudden recurrence of severe unilateral scrotal pain, significant swelling, and a low-grade fever. Urgent after-hours assessment was critically mandated to physically inspect the surgical wound, exclude a rapidly expanding, compressive scrotal haematoma or aggressive cellulitis, and prevent potential ischemic compromise of the newly salvaged testicle before routine daytime services were available.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My son had surgery to untwist his testicle four days ago, but tonight the right side has suddenly swollen up to the size of a lemon and is throbbing terribly.
History of Present Illness: The patient is status post urgent surgical exploration and bilateral testicular orchiopexy performed four days ago for acute testicular torsion. They had been experiencing resolving, mild post-operative discomfort until this evening. Over the past four hours, they developed a sudden, acute exacerbation of severe, throbbing pain localized to the right hemiscrotum, accompanied by rapid, moderate to severe localized scrotal swelling and induration. They report a low-grade subjective fever and mild nausea secondary to the pain. They confirm the surgical incision has been kept clean and dry. There is an absence of purulent discharge. Normal urinary function is present without dysuria.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently restricted to bed, exhibiting an antalgic gait when attempting to walk to the bathroom.
Social/Safety Concerns: High parental anxiety regarding the survival of the salvaged testicle.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/75 | HR: 98 | Temp: 37.6C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, guarding the pelvic area, anxious but not appearing systemically toxic.
Physical Exam: Genitourinary: A healing surgical incision is visible transversely on the scrotum, with intact sutures, no wound dehiscence, and no purulent discharge. However, the right hemiscrotum is significantly swollen, erythematous, and highly indurated. The testicle is palpable and fixed securely within the dependent portion of the scrotum, but the overlying tissue and tunica vaginalis space are tense and exquisitely tender, consistent with a developing postoperative haematoma or reactive inflammatory effusion. The cremasteric reflex is absent, typical post-surgical fixation. Expected investigation results would demonstrate normal, robust, and symmetrical bilateral testicular arterial blood flow on a follow-up colour Doppler scrotal ultrasound, ruling out recurrent ischemia.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment; the patient is appropriately resting in bed.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking prescribed postoperative paracetamol and ibuprofen regularly.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Testicular Torsion (Post-op) - Status: Exacerbated (Suspected Postoperative Scrotal Haematoma/Reactive Swelling)
Differentials:

Recurrent Testicular Torsion - Ruled out/unlikely due to the recent secure bilateral surgical fixation (orchiopexy) and the presence of generalized induration/swelling in the tissue planes rather than an isolated, high-riding, ischemic testicle.

Surgical Site Infection (Cellulitis) - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary cause due to the lack of high systemic fever, lack of spreading erythema beyond the scrotum, and a clean, non-purulent incision line.

Epididymo-orchitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the immediate postoperative timeframe making mechanical bleeding or reactive fluid collection vastly more probable than a new ascending bacterial infection.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, highly distressing exacerbation of pain and swelling shortly after a major scrotal surgery. While recurrent torsion is anatomically unlikely due to the recent orchiopexy, the rapid accumulation of fluid or blood (haematoma) in the confined scrotal space causes severe capsular stretch pain mimicking the original ischemic event. An urgent after-hours physical examination was absolutely necessary to inspect the wound for dehiscence or gross purulence, and to palpate the induration. Confirming the absence of overt sepsis allowed for immediate conservative management and scheduled urgent imaging, rather than an immediate return to the operating theatre.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed the immediate application of wrapped ice packs to the scrotum for 15 minutes every hour to induce local vasoconstriction and halt any ongoing micro-vascular bleeding. Advised strict bed rest with the scrotum continuously elevated on a rolled towel to promote venous and lymphatic drainage. Ordered an urgent morning Scrotal Doppler Ultrasound to definitively rule out recurrent torsion and quantify the volume of the suspected haematoma.
Positive Behavioral Change: To maximize the reduction of swelling, the patient is instructed to maintain strict scrotal elevation by placing a rolled hand towel under the scrotum whenever lying down. By immediately feeling a reduction in the heavy, dragging, throbbing pain, the patient receives strong positive reinforcement to maintain this specific mechanical elevation habit, drastically accelerating the resolution of the reactive oedema.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None required immediately.
DME/Safety (Optional): Wrapped ice packs and supportive athletic underwear recommended.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review tomorrow afternoon to review the Doppler ultrasound findings and liaise with the operating surgical team.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate return to the Emergency Department via ambulance is required if the surgical wound bursts open (dehiscence), if thick yellow pus begins draining from the incision, or if the patient develops a high fever with uncontrollable shivering.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The swelling is highly likely a collection of blood or fluid from the surgery reacting to movement, but an ultrasound is absolutely required to guarantee blood flow to the testicle remains normal.
Clinical Course: The throbbing pain should improve over the next 24 hours with strict rest and elevation. The hard swelling and bruising may take several weeks to completely resorb and fade.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If the surgical cut bursts open, starts leaking thick pus, or if you suddenly spike a high fever, you must go directly to the Emergency Department as this indicates a severe infection.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: After your surgery, the tissues inside your scrotum were inflamed. A small blood vessel has likely oozed, creating a pocket of blood (haematoma) around the testicle. Because the space is tight, this swelling stretches the tissue, causing severe throbbing pain that feels terrifyingly like the original twisting. Strict rest, ice, and elevating the area will stop the oozing and let the fluid drain away.
Actionable Steps: Stay completely in bed. Keep a rolled towel under the scrotum at all times to lift it up against gravity. Apply ice for 15 minutes every hour. Attend the urgent ultrasound tomorrow morning.

